WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.609
In this lesson you'll learn how old

2
00:00:03.609 --> 00:00:06.000
the tools you build with really are,

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.122
and how fast each part of them is still changing.

4
00:00:10.122 --> 00:00:12.386
Most of it is decades old,

5
00:00:12.386 --> 00:00:14.789
but old doesn't mean frozen:

6
00:00:14.789 --> 00:00:17.645
everything moves at one of three speeds.

7
00:00:17.645 --> 00:00:22.022
The ideas barely move, so you learn them once.

8
00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.764
The versions change every year,

9
00:00:23.764 --> 00:00:25.819
so you check which one you have.

10
00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:28.977
Security changes fastest, so assume

11
00:00:28.977 --> 00:00:32.564
what you learned a few years ago is out of date.

12
00:00:32.564 --> 00:00:35.351
And AI, the newest layer on top,

13
00:00:35.351 --> 00:00:38.545
changes fastest of all.

14
00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:42.350
On the course map, this lesson sits at the Start,

15
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.858
in Orientation: what this course is,

16
00:00:44.858 --> 00:00:46.890
and the lantern you carry.

17
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.723
Every district ahead is an old street,

18
00:00:49.723 --> 00:00:52.532
laid long before AI arrived.

19
00:00:52.532 --> 00:00:55.481
Versions and packages come later,

20
00:00:55.481 --> 00:00:59.080
in the Code district; git, in History.

21
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:02.912
The fastest-changing part, logins and locks,

22
00:01:02.912 --> 00:01:06.046
gets a district of its own: Trust.

23
00:01:06.046 --> 00:01:09.053
And the lanterns, the AI, were strung up last,

24
00:01:09.053 --> 00:01:11.667
and they're still being rehung.

25
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:14.485
Aaron, who made this course, has a degree

26
00:01:14.485 --> 00:01:17.295
in Information Systems and Data Science.

27
00:01:17.295 --> 00:01:19.338
He still wrote Python in Notepad,

28
00:01:19.338 --> 00:01:22.845
and saved it as a .txt file.

29
00:01:22.845 --> 00:01:25.643
He didn't know to rename it to .py,

30
00:01:25.643 --> 00:01:29.439
or to open a terminal and cd into the folder.

31
00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:31.959
He'd never heard of .venv.

32
00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:33.538
None of that was new.

33
00:01:33.538 --> 00:01:36.800
Notepad came with the first Windows, in 1985.

34
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.739
Python is from 1991.

35
00:01:38.739 --> 00:01:41.665
The Unix shell, from 1971.

36
00:01:41.665 --> 00:01:46.344
And the tool behind .venv joined Python in 2012.

37
00:01:46.344 --> 00:01:48.143
Aaron wasn't behind on the latest thing.

38
00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:49.861
He was missing the oldest things,

39
00:01:49.861 --> 00:01:52.870
the ones everyone assumes you already have.

40
00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.655
Picture one long street,

41
00:01:54.655 --> 00:01:56.617
with a year painted on every building.

42
00:01:56.617 --> 00:01:58.997
1969: Unix.

43
00:01:58.997 --> 00:02:01.029
1973: Ethernet.

44
00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:04.257
1981: MS-DOS.

45
00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:06.950
1983: DNS, the internet's address book.

46
00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:10.085
Then the web, Python and Linux, all around 1991.

47
00:02:10.085 --> 00:02:11.641
JavaScript, 1995.

48
00:02:11.641 --> 00:02:13.231
Git, 2005.

49
00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:14.450
GitHub, 2008.

50
00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.809
Docker, 2013.

51
00:02:15.809 --> 00:02:17.422
VS Code, 2015.

52
00:02:17.422 --> 00:02:18.955
And only at the very end of the street,

53
00:02:18.955 --> 00:02:21.706
the lanterns. GitHub Copilot, 2021.

54
00:02:21.706 --> 00:02:24.307
ChatGPT, late 2022.

55
00:02:24.307 --> 00:02:27.836
MCP, a standard way to plug AI into other tools,

56
00:02:27.836 --> 00:02:29.601
November 2024.

57
00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:31.168
More than fifty years of street.

58
00:02:31.168 --> 00:02:33.190
A few years of lanterns.

59
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.438
Every year on that street is a birthday,

60
00:02:36.438 --> 00:02:37.994
not a finish line.

61
00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:41.128
Look closely at one, and it has three parts

62
00:02:41.128 --> 00:02:43.323
that age at different speeds.

63
00:02:43.323 --> 00:02:47.270
The stone is the idea: a file, a commit,

64
00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.928
a message sent across a network.

65
00:02:49.928 --> 00:02:51.542
That barely moves.

66
00:02:51.542 --> 00:02:55.896
The paint is the version, the number on the sign.

67
00:02:55.896 --> 00:02:58.775
It gets redone every year or so.

68
00:02:58.775 --> 00:03:01.805
And the lock on the door is security:

69
00:03:01.805 --> 00:03:03.222
how you prove who you are,

70
00:03:03.222 --> 00:03:05.625
and how secrets get scrambled.

71
00:03:05.625 --> 00:03:08.133
Locks get replaced fastest,

72
00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:11.002
because burglars keep learning.

73
00:03:11.453 --> 00:03:13.183
Start with the paint.

74
00:03:13.183 --> 00:03:16.759
Git is from 2005, and it still ships

75
00:03:16.759 --> 00:03:19.104
new versions several times a year.

76
00:03:19.104 --> 00:03:23.272
In 2019 it got git switch and git restore,

77
00:03:23.272 --> 00:03:26.895
clearer ways to do jobs that checkout still does.

78
00:03:26.895 --> 00:03:29.669
In 2020, GitHub started calling the first

79
00:03:29.669 --> 00:03:33.326
branch of new projects main instead of master.

80
00:03:33.326 --> 00:03:35.857
Python ships a new version every October,

81
00:03:35.857 --> 00:03:39.015
and Python 2 was retired in 2020.

82
00:03:39.015 --> 00:03:43.345
In 2024, Python 3.13 brought an experimental

83
00:03:43.345 --> 00:03:45.876
build that can run a program's threads

84
00:03:45.876 --> 00:03:48.651
on several processor cores at once.

85
00:03:48.651 --> 00:03:52.053
VS Code used to ship a new release every month.

86
00:03:52.053 --> 00:03:55.675
In 2026, it went to every week.

87
00:03:55.675 --> 00:03:57.034
Same stone.

88
00:03:57.034 --> 00:03:59.868
Fresh paint, over and over.

89
00:04:00.318 --> 00:04:01.734
Then there are packages,

90
00:04:01.734 --> 00:04:04.139
code other people wrote for you to use.

91
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.888
npm for JavaScript and PyPI for Python

92
00:04:07.888 --> 00:04:10.442
are markets that never close:

93
00:04:10.442 --> 00:04:13.901
new packages and new versions arrive every day.

94
00:04:13.901 --> 00:04:17.187
In a version number like 2.4.1,

95
00:04:17.187 --> 00:04:19.137
a change to the first part warns

96
00:04:19.137 --> 00:04:21.192
you that something may break.

97
00:04:21.192 --> 00:04:22.609
A lockfile writes down

98
00:04:22.609 --> 00:04:24.513
the exact versions that worked,

99
00:04:24.513 --> 00:04:28.054
so your project doesn't quietly change under you.

100
00:04:28.054 --> 00:04:30.841
And attackers found the market too:

101
00:04:30.841 --> 00:04:32.640
they publish look-alike names,

102
00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.869
or take over real packages.

103
00:04:34.869 --> 00:04:36.855
So careful people pin their versions

104
00:04:36.855 --> 00:04:38.598
and update on purpose.

105
00:04:39.316 --> 00:04:41.359
Even the tools that install packages

106
00:04:41.359 --> 00:04:45.190
keep changing: pip, then Poetry in 2018,

107
00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.616
then uv in 2024, all still in use.

108
00:04:50.065 --> 00:04:52.967
Now the locks, which change fastest.

109
00:04:52.967 --> 00:04:55.882
For decades, a password alone opened the door.

110
00:04:55.882 --> 00:04:58.134
Passwords leaked and got reused,

111
00:04:58.134 --> 00:05:00.328
so sites added two factor:

112
00:05:00.328 --> 00:05:03.405
something you know, plus something you have.

113
00:05:03.405 --> 00:05:06.122
The usual second factor was a code

114
00:05:06.122 --> 00:05:08.281
sent by text message.

115
00:05:08.281 --> 00:05:10.324
Many sites are moving away from it,

116
00:05:10.324 --> 00:05:12.170
because of SIM swapping:

117
00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.388
someone talks your phone company into moving

118
00:05:14.388 --> 00:05:16.211
your number onto their phone,

119
00:05:16.211 --> 00:05:18.335
and your codes go with it.

120
00:05:18.335 --> 00:05:24.152
In 2017, NIST, America's standards agency,

121
00:05:24.152 --> 00:05:26.532
restricted text message codes.

122
00:05:26.532 --> 00:05:29.203
So the codes moved into authenticator apps,

123
00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:30.909
like Google Authenticator,

124
00:05:30.909 --> 00:05:34.079
Microsoft Authenticator or Authy.

125
00:05:34.079 --> 00:05:36.401
They make a fresh code every thirty seconds,

126
00:05:36.401 --> 00:05:39.385
from a standard published in 2011.

127
00:05:39.385 --> 00:05:41.951
Some apps skip the code and just ask:

128
00:05:41.951 --> 00:05:43.682
was this you?

129
00:05:44.132 --> 00:05:45.595
The next lock trusts

130
00:05:45.595 --> 00:05:48.312
your device instead of a code.

131
00:05:48.312 --> 00:05:51.864
A passkey keeps a secret on your phone or laptop.

132
00:05:51.864 --> 00:05:54.093
You unlock it with your face,

133
00:05:54.093 --> 00:05:56.543
your fingerprint or a PIN,

134
00:05:56.543 --> 00:05:59.387
and the secret is never sent to the site,

135
00:05:59.387 --> 00:06:03.102
so a fake look-alike site has nothing to steal.

136
00:06:03.102 --> 00:06:06.400
The web standard behind it was finished in 2019,

137
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.708
and in 2022 Apple, Google

138
00:06:09.708 --> 00:06:13.400
and Microsoft committed to passkeys together.

139
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.467
The goal is no password at all.

140
00:06:15.467 --> 00:06:18.462
And sites stopped asking nicely.

141
00:06:18.462 --> 00:06:21.190
GitHub stopped accepting account passwords

142
00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:26.287
for git in 2021, and in 2023 it began requiring

143
00:06:26.287 --> 00:06:29.712
two factor from everyone who contributes code.

144
00:06:29.712 --> 00:06:31.500
A lock you learned a few years ago

145
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:34.311
may already be the old lock.

146
00:06:34.762 --> 00:06:37.525
Locks also moved off the door.

147
00:06:37.525 --> 00:06:41.055
Instead of every site keeping its own passwords,

148
00:06:41.055 --> 00:06:44.073
many hand sign-in to a dedicated service:

149
00:06:44.073 --> 00:06:45.397
Sign in with Google,

150
00:06:45.397 --> 00:06:48.195
or a company's identity provider.

151
00:06:48.195 --> 00:06:50.598
Now there's a service for everything:

152
00:06:50.598 --> 00:06:55.393
one for logins, one for secrets, one for keys.

153
00:06:55.393 --> 00:06:57.321
Aaron met this at work.

154
00:06:57.321 --> 00:06:58.772
His automations were tied

155
00:06:58.772 --> 00:07:00.351
to his personal accounts,

156
00:07:00.351 --> 00:07:03.056
and they had to move to service accounts,

157
00:07:03.056 --> 00:07:04.809
Windows Credential Manager

158
00:07:04.809 --> 00:07:09.163
and app registrations in Microsoft Entra.

159
00:07:09.163 --> 00:07:12.112
A login for the job, not the person.

160
00:07:12.112 --> 00:07:14.771
Even that name moved: Entra was called

161
00:07:14.771 --> 00:07:18.766
Azure Active Directory until 2023.

162
00:07:19.216 --> 00:07:21.468
Inside every lock is the metal:

163
00:07:21.468 --> 00:07:23.082
the math that scrambles data

164
00:07:23.082 --> 00:07:25.253
and checks that it wasn't changed.

165
00:07:25.253 --> 00:07:27.889
That wears out too, as computers get faster

166
00:07:27.889 --> 00:07:30.257
and researchers find shortcuts.

167
00:07:30.257 --> 00:07:35.771
MD5, a fingerprint for files, was broken in 2004.

168
00:07:35.771 --> 00:07:39.545
SHA-1 fell in 2017, when researchers made two

169
00:07:39.545 --> 00:07:41.704
different files with the same fingerprint.

170
00:07:41.704 --> 00:07:43.864
The old versions of TLS,

171
00:07:43.864 --> 00:07:48.589
the lock behind HTTPS, were retired in 2021.

172
00:07:48.589 --> 00:07:52.977
In 2024, NIST published new standards built

173
00:07:52.977 --> 00:07:55.067
to survive future quantum computers.

174
00:07:55.067 --> 00:07:58.864
Even git, which names every commit with SHA-1,

175
00:07:58.864 --> 00:08:02.277
has started moving to SHA-256.

176
00:08:02.277 --> 00:08:04.262
Here's where the street picture breaks.

177
00:08:04.262 --> 00:08:07.177
On a real street, an old lock has charm.

178
00:08:07.177 --> 00:08:10.160
In software, an old lock may be one

179
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.786
somebody has already learned to pick.

180
00:08:13.236 --> 00:08:16.382
Here's what the speeds look like up close.

181
00:08:16.382 --> 00:08:18.669
A tutorial's command fails,

182
00:08:18.669 --> 00:08:22.094
or one of its options no longer exists:

183
00:08:22.094 --> 00:08:24.846
the paint changed after it was written.

184
00:08:24.846 --> 00:08:28.341
One project says master and another says main:

185
00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:32.416
same idea, a default GitHub changed in 2020.

186
00:08:32.416 --> 00:08:35.156
The word deprecated means this still works,

187
00:08:35.156 --> 00:08:36.885
but it's on its way out.

188
00:08:36.885 --> 00:08:40.763
It's a warning, not an error, so plan to move.

189
00:08:40.763 --> 00:08:44.095
An old answer where print has no parentheses

190
00:08:44.095 --> 00:08:46.231
is Python 2.

191
00:08:46.231 --> 00:08:49.366
And one day a site you've used for years

192
00:08:49.366 --> 00:08:51.595
won't let you in until you set up

193
00:08:51.595 --> 00:08:55.159
an authenticator app or a passkey.

194
00:08:55.159 --> 00:08:57.423
You didn't do anything wrong.

195
00:08:57.423 --> 00:08:59.979
The lock got replaced.

196
00:09:00.429 --> 00:09:04.632
AI is the newest layer, and the fastest moving.

197
00:09:04.632 --> 00:09:06.664
New models arrive every few months,

198
00:09:06.664 --> 00:09:08.916
and old ones get switched off.

199
00:09:08.916 --> 00:09:11.540
And an AI learned from a snapshot

200
00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:14.721
that stops where its training ended.

201
00:09:14.721 --> 00:09:17.508
So it may suggest an old package version,

202
00:09:17.508 --> 00:09:19.110
a retired command,

203
00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:22.163
or a login method a site no longer accepts.

204
00:09:22.163 --> 00:09:25.623
A coding agent still works on the old street:

205
00:09:25.623 --> 00:09:30.917
it runs cd, makes a .venv, saves with git.

206
00:09:30.917 --> 00:09:33.784
When it reaches for the paint or the locks,

207
00:09:33.784 --> 00:09:37.175
ask which version it's assuming, and check.

208
00:09:37.175 --> 00:09:40.251
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

209
00:09:40.251 --> 00:09:43.179
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.

210
00:09:43.629 --> 00:09:45.754
You don't need to memorize these dates,

211
00:09:45.754 --> 00:09:48.772
or which algorithm replaced which.

212
00:09:48.772 --> 00:09:51.141
You don't need to chase every release either;

213
00:09:51.141 --> 00:09:53.823
most updates will never touch you.

214
00:09:53.823 --> 00:09:57.828
Security updates are the exception: take those.

215
00:09:57.828 --> 00:10:01.230
Keep one question for anything new you meet:

216
00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.146
is this street, or lantern?

217
00:10:03.146 --> 00:10:05.735
And if it's street, which part?

218
00:10:05.735 --> 00:10:08.614
Stone: learn it once.

219
00:10:08.614 --> 00:10:10.797
Paint: check the version.

220
00:10:10.797 --> 00:10:13.931
Lock: assume it has changed.

221
00:10:13.931 --> 00:10:16.487
Lantern: check the date.

222
00:10:16.937 --> 00:10:18.458
Three things to keep.

223
00:10:18.458 --> 00:10:20.699
Learn the old ideas once.

224
00:10:20.699 --> 00:10:22.138
Files, the shell,

225
00:10:22.138 --> 00:10:25.052
the web and git had decades to settle.

226
00:10:25.052 --> 00:10:26.132
Check the version,

227
00:10:26.132 --> 00:10:28.431
and assume the lock has changed.

228
00:10:28.431 --> 00:10:31.031
Versions move every year,

229
00:10:31.031 --> 00:10:33.795
and security moves faster still.

230
00:10:33.795 --> 00:10:36.407
Check the date on anything AI tells you.

231
00:10:36.407 --> 00:10:39.472
It is the newest and fastest of all,

232
00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:42.132
especially about versions and locks.
